  </div>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Graphic design ceiling shape</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Wider independent-income door: freelance, a small studio, or agency ownership are common exits with real client work in hand.</li>
        <li>Ceiling rises fastest for designers who add strategic brand or marketing thinking, not just tighter execution.</li>
        <li>Growth is more reputation- and portfolio-driven than credential- or ladder-driven.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">UI/UX design ceiling shape</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Structural corporate ladder: junior to senior to lead to head of design is a defined path inside most tech and product companies.</li>
        <li>Ceiling rises fastest for designers who can tie their work to measurable product outcomes and influence cross-functional decisions.</li>
        <li>Independent income (freelance or consulting) is realistic but usually needs more in-house years first to earn client trust.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither ceiling is objectively higher in every sense. UI/UX's ceiling is higher in raw pay and more
    predictable in structure; graphic design's ceiling is pulled up by independence and personal brand.
    Pick based on which kind of growth you would rather spend a decade building.
  </p>

