      If neither pure lane feels like a complete fit after the checkpoints in this article, the overlap
      lane is often the more realistic first target than forcing yourself into either extreme — a
      graphic designer who learns Figma and basic wireframing, or a UI/UX designer who leans into visual
      systems work, becomes more valuable than someone who only knows one side.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is what unlocking high income opportunities actually looks like in design</strong> —
      not picking the "richer" title, but stacking a skill mix (craft, research literacy, communication,
      and a niche) that neither pure lane teaches on its own.
    </p>
  </div>

