  <h2 id="gates">Pass The 3 Gates before you spend real time or money on this</h2>

  <p>
    The 4-Checkpoint Protocol tells you which lane fits on paper. The 3 Gates make you test it in the
    real world before you commit a course fee, a career switch, or a full year to it.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">
      Do not commit to a full course or a mid-career switch before passing all three gates in your chosen
      lane.
    </p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
        <p>
          For graphic design, design one real brand identity piece — even a logo and social kit for a
          friend's small business — and finish it to a genuinely presentable standard. For UI/UX, take
          one real, flawed screen (an app you use and dislike) and redesign one flow, then test it on
          three real people.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of communication</span>
        <p>
          Explain in under two minutes, in plain language, why your design decisions were the right
          ones. If you can only point at how it looks, not why it works, you are not ready to sell this
          in an interview.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of value</span>
        <p>
          Show the work to a working graphic designer or UI/UX designer and ask one direct question:
          "Would this get shortlisted at your company?" Use their answer, not your own hope, to decide.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="switching-later">Can you switch between them later?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes, and the switch happens constantly through the overlap roles described above, which is one more
    reason not to treat this as a permanent, irreversible fork in the road.
  </p>

  <p>
    Graphic designers moving toward UI/UX usually need a focused stretch of Figma, basic research
    methods, and information architecture study, plus one real case study applying it to an actual
    product, before the switch becomes credible to employers. Their existing layout thinking and visual
    hierarchy sense is a real head start; what they are missing is research discipline and process, not
    visual taste.
  </p>

  <p>
    UI/UX designers moving toward graphic or brand design usually need to rebuild speed and comfort with
    pure visual craft across print and static formats, since designing a tested product flow is a
    different skill from producing a striking, standalone visual piece under a tight deadline.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Neither switch is instant, and neither switch is rare. Treat your first choice as a strong starting
      lane, not a life sentence — the overlap lane between these two careers is wider than most
      comparison charts suggest.
    </p>
  </div>

